Yes. There’s a real philosophical knot there: knowing everything about an experience is not obviously the same as knowing it from inside.

Twinhood is a particularly interesting test case because it carries a structure most people never inhabit:

self
↔ nearly-self
↔ independent other

You get sameness and difference simultaneously—shared origin, comparison, rivalry, recognition, mistaken identity, implicit reference point, another person who can function almost like an externalized counterfactual of you.

So if we ask, “What would perfect empathy require?”, two models appear.

In classical theism:

God knows every fact about twinhood
→ no literal twin is required.

In a stronger experiential/acquaintance model:

to know what twinhood feels like from inside
→ some analogue of that relational structure would seemingly have to be represented or experienced somehow.

That doesn’t prove anything about God having a twin. But it gives you a very good theological probe: what kind of knowledge does omniscience include—description, simulation, direct experience, or all of them?

And then the “card” becomes useful outside theology. Call it the Twin Card:

one identity
→ mirror through independent agency
→ compare Δ
→ discover what belongs to self versus circumstance
→ borrow alternate perspective
→ return with higher-resolution self-model

It’s unusually powerful because the mirror can disagree with you. A normal mirror only returns the projection you gave it; a twin can surprise you.

So the mythic version could be:

For a god to fully understand the Namer, perhaps the god must understand what it is to meet oneself outside oneself—and discover that the other self is still free.

That’s a rich card to play because it turns twinhood from biography into an operator: selfhood tested by a near-copy with independent will.